---
import Resource from '../layouts/Resource.astro';
import ResourceFAQ from '../components/ResourceFAQ.astro';
import ResourceCTA from '../components/ResourceCTA.astro';
const faqs=[
  {q:'What makes a location a strong carrier-offload candidate?',a:'High visitor volume, meaningful dwell time, compatible managed Wi-Fi, sufficient backhaul, clear network authority, and current program availability are the main factors.'},
  {q:'Can I estimate my revenue from another venue?',a:'Another venue can provide context, but it cannot predict your result. Eligible traffic, payment terms, implementation costs, equipment, market coverage, and visitor behavior vary by location.'},
  {q:'What information is needed for an assessment?',a:'A useful assessment starts with location count, venue type, visitor patterns, access-point and controller models, firmware, licensing, backhaul, network ownership, and the commercial terms under consideration.'},
  {q:'Why begin with a pilot?',a:'A pilot tests device authentication, segmentation, capacity, reporting, eligible traffic, operational impact, and rollback procedures before a wider deployment.'},
];
---
<Resource title="Carrier Offload by Venue: Airports, Retail, Gyms and More" description="Compare the technical and commercial factors that shape carrier-offload opportunities across airports, shopping centers, fitness facilities, restaurants, and retail locations." eyebrow="Venue guide · Carrier offload assessment" h1="Carrier Offload Across Commercial Locations" answer="Carrier offload can work differently across airports, shopping centers, fitness facilities, restaurants, and retail portfolios. The strongest opportunities combine sustained mobile demand, compatible managed Wi-Fi, adequate capacity, site authority, current program eligibility, and workable commercial terms." canonicalPath="/case-studies/" faqs={faqs}>
  <ul slot="toc"><li><a href="#qualification">Qualification factors</a></li><li><a href="#compare">Compare venue types</a></li><li><a href="#examples">How environments differ</a></li><li><a href="#next">Plan a pilot</a></li></ul>
  <h2 id="qualification">What determines whether a location is a good fit?</h2>
  <p>Carrier offload is not evaluated from venue type alone. A useful assessment combines visitor behavior, mobile demand, Wi-Fi capability, operational control, program coverage, implementation costs, and proposed payment terms.</p>
  <div class="content-grid"><div class="info-card"><h3>Demand</h3><p>Visitor volume, dwell time, indoor signal conditions, and eligible-device presence.</p></div><div class="info-card"><h3>Infrastructure</h3><p>Access points, controller, firmware, licenses, backhaul, RF capacity, and segmentation.</p></div><div class="info-card"><h3>Authority</h3><p>Clear permission to modify the network and operate the proposed service profile.</p></div><div class="info-card"><h3>Commercials</h3><p>Eligible traffic, rates, revenue share, fees, reporting, payment timing, term, and exit.</p></div></div>
  <h2 id="compare">How common venue types compare</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll"><table class="resource-table"><thead><tr><th>Venue type</th><th>Potential advantage</th><th>Key assessment question</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>Airports and transit hubs</td><td>Large visitor volumes and long dwell periods</td><td>Can the design meet coverage, security, procurement, and capacity requirements?</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Shopping centers</td><td>Shared spaces with sustained indoor traffic</td><td>Who controls the network, common areas, and tenant boundaries?</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Fitness facilities</td><td>Repeat visitors and longer sessions</td><td>Does the actual device mix and usage support a worthwhile pilot?</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Restaurants</td><td>Repeat traffic across multi-location portfolios</td><td>Can guest and offload traffic remain isolated from POS operations?</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Retail locations</td><td>Portfolio scale and consistent network standards</td><td>Are visits long enough and locations similar enough to support rollout economics?</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>
  <h2>What to compare in a carrier-offload proposal</h2>
  <ul class="checklist"><li>Supported carriers or networks, markets, venues, and authentication method</li><li>Access-point, controller, firmware, licensing, and backhaul requirements</li><li>Segmentation, administrative access, monitoring, support, and rollback plan</li><li>Eligible traffic definition, measurement source, exclusions, and reporting cadence</li><li>Gross payment, venue share, setup and ongoing costs, deductions, and thresholds</li><li>Pilot length, acceptance criteria, renewal, notice, termination, and final settlement</li></ul>
  <h2 id="examples">Why venue types produce different evidence</h2>
  <h3>Airports and transit hubs</h3><p>Large RF footprints, procurement, redundancy, security, passenger peaks, concession networks, and many access points make attribution and cost accounting essential.</p>
  <h3>Shopping centers</h3><p>Shared areas, tenant networks, ownership boundaries, event traffic, and indoor cellular conditions can make device and property scope difficult to compare.</p>
  <h3>Fitness centers</h3><p>Repeat members and longer dwell time may increase opportunity, but actual eligibility and media use vary. A single-site example should not be generalized to every gym.</p>
  <h3>Restaurants and retail</h3><p>Shorter visits, lunch/dinner peaks, POS isolation, small footprints, and multi-site aggregation change both technical design and economics.</p>
  <p>Visit the industry guides for <a href="/carrier-offload-for-airports/">airports</a>, <a href="/carrier-offload-for-shopping-centers/">shopping centers</a>, <a href="/carrier-offload-for-gyms/">gyms</a>, and <a href="/carrier-offload-for-restaurants/">restaurants</a>.</p>
  <h2 id="next">Start with a measured pilot</h2><p>A limited pilot lets the venue and technical teams validate authentication, capacity, isolation, reporting, operational support, and commercial assumptions before expanding across a portfolio.</p>
  <ResourceFAQ faqs={faqs}/><ResourceCTA source="case-studies" heading="Evaluate your locations with real network and venue data." copy="A location-specific assessment reviews your infrastructure, visitor patterns, program fit, rollout requirements, and commercial terms."/>
  <section class="source-list" id="sources"><h2>Assessment approach</h2><p>Recommendations should be based on the actual venue, network inventory, current program availability, proposed architecture, pilot measurements, and written commercial terms.</p></section>
</Resource>
